---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="The magic glyph keyboard"
  description="The 377-icon vocabulary the player uses to cast spells, plus how we matched each glyph to its English meaning."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Names & Words', href: `${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/` },
    { label: 'Magic glyphs' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Magic in this game isn't cast by selecting from a menu. The player
    composes a spell by picking icons from a keyboard of cute pictograms
    — each picture is a word in the game's "magic language."
  </p>

  <p>
    The keyboard holds <strong>377 populated glyphs</strong> out of 1000
    slots. The slot table is in <code>2d/inputmagic/msg.ofs</code>; the
    visual icons are in the surrounding files
    (<code>mgcall.ofs</code> and the <code>mgc01..09c.lzs</code> family),
    all sharing a palette in <code>mgc80.ofs</code> — see
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/palettes/`}>finding the right
    palette</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What the glyphs are</h2>
  <p>
    Each glyph is one word, in three rough categories:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Common nouns and adjectives</strong> — "magic," "yes,"
      "love," "morning," "river," "flower," "Tower," "Beach."</li>
    <li><strong>Character names</strong> — every NPC has a glyph.
      Vivian, Mr. Vlad, Mr. Oakley, Miss Strawe, Mr. Graves, and so on.</li>
    <li><strong>Numbers and symbols</strong> — 0 through 9, exclamation
      and question marks.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Spells are formed by chaining glyphs together. "Greet" + "morning"
    + "[NPC name]" is a greeting spell. The game parses these compositions
    and triggers different effects depending on the combination.
  </p>

  <h2>How we got the English names</h2>
  <p>
    Tongari Boushi never released in English, but the magic-glyph idea
    came from the same studio's earlier game,
    <em>Magician's Quest: Mysterious Times</em> (called <em>Enchanted
    Folk and the School of Wizardry</em> in Europe), which <em>did</em>
    release in English. Both games share a "magic word" vocabulary, and
    Game 1's English localization gives us official English equivalents
    for 280 of the 377 glyphs in Tongari.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Source</th><th>Count</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Konami official canon</td>
        <td>280</td>
        <td>Direct from Magician's Quest 1 English release.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>NPC database (hand-curated)</td>
        <td>51</td>
        <td>Character names we'd already locked down for this project.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>NPC placeholder</td>
        <td>2</td>
        <td>Names where the database entry exists but isn't yet
          translated.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Universal / dictionary</td>
        <td>16</td>
        <td>Obvious dictionary words ("ice," "school," "town").</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Fresh translations</td>
        <td>28</td>
        <td>Game-3-only content with no Game 1 parallel; we picked
          translations from context.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Total: 377 glyphs, 377 translations. Coverage is 100 percent —
    every populated slot has an English form.
  </p>

  <h2>Sample glyphs</h2>
  <p>A small slice of what the keyboard looks like:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Index</th><th>Japanese</th><th>English</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>あいさつ</td><td>greeting</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>まほう</td><td>magic</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>はい</td><td>yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>ちがう</td><td>no</td></tr>
      <tr><td>13</td><td>ラブ</td><td>love</td></tr>
      <tr><td>15</td><td>おおきい</td><td>big</td></tr>
      <tr><td>17</td><td>はやい</td><td>fast</td></tr>
      <tr><td>34</td><td>ともだち</td><td>friend</td></tr>
      <tr><td>41</td><td>こうちょう</td><td>Principal Sol</td></tr>
      <tr><td>42</td><td>リカルド</td><td>Mr. Vlad</td></tr>
      <tr><td>44</td><td>ミスキーキー</td><td>Miss Strawe</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Why glyph consistency matters in dialog</h2>
  <p>
    When an NPC's dialog mentions a glyph word — like a hint that says
    "try using the <em>morning</em> magic word" — the original
    Japanese uses a colored highlight around the glyph's name. Our
    translation needs to match that highlighted word to the glyph
    exactly, or the player won't know which icon to look for.
  </p>

  <p>
    To catch mismatches we run a dialog audit that scans every
    color-highlighted span in the dialog database and checks whether
    the English text matches the glyph's canonical English. About
    2,500 entries match cleanly; 700 had inconsistencies that we
    then fixed by canonicalizing the dialog to the glyph's official
    name.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
